counterfactuals.output:
The study examined how reliably the CKM phase \(\alpha\) can be extracted from the branching ratios and time-dependent CP asymmetries of \(B\to\pi\pi\) decays using the Gronau–London isospin analysis.

Under isospin symmetry, the amplitudes for \(B^0\to\pi^+\pi^-\), \(B^0\to\pi^0\pi^0\), and \(B^+\to\pi^+\pi^0\), together with their CP conjugates, form two isospin triangles. Their relative orientation determines \(\alpha\), while allowing the poorly known QCD-penguin amplitudes to be fitted from data. The construction generally produces several discrete “mirror” solutions and can become weakly constrained when the triangles are nearly degenerate.

A central point was the statistical disagreement between frequentist and Bayesian analyses. In a frequentist treatment, hadronic amplitudes are nuisance parameters that are profiled over, producing confidence-level curves with multiple allowed regions or broad plateaus. Bayesian marginalization instead assigns priors to these amplitudes. Because flat priors are not invariant under changes of amplitude parametrization, different—but physically equivalent—choices of hadronic variables can generate noticeably different posterior distributions for \(\alpha\). Peaks may therefore reflect prior volume or parametrization choices rather than information supplied by the data. The study argued that numerical Bayesian bounds must be quoted together with their prior and parametrization assumptions, whereas the frequentist construction is less sensitive to such choices, although it can yield less visually decisive constraints.

The analysis also allowed New Physics in \(\Delta I=1/2\) amplitudes, the sector containing the QCD penguin. Such contributions can carry new weak phases, but reparametrization invariance allows them to be absorbed into effective hadronic amplitudes. Consequently:

- the isospin determination of \(\alpha\) remains valid if New Physics is restricted to \(\Delta I=1/2\);
- \(B\to\pi\pi\) data alone generally cannot establish or uniquely characterize that New Physics;
- sensitivity requires an independent determination of \(\alpha\), plus theoretical information about the Standard-Model hadronic amplitudes;
- New Physics in \(\Delta I=3/2\), or sizable isospin-breaking/electroweak-penguin effects, would be more dangerous because it can directly contaminate the isospin construction.

Overall, the study presented \(B\to\pi\pi\) as a theoretically clean route to \(\alpha\) under the isospin assumptions, but emphasized that the apparent precision and even the shape of the inferred probability distribution can depend strongly on statistical methodology. It also showed that clean phase extraction does not automatically imply strong sensitivity to New Physics in the \(\Delta I=1/2\) penguin sector.
